WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:09.783
Respiratory drugs are split into three parts, and this second part covers sedation, paralysis and pharmacokinetics.

2
00:00:09.783 --> 00:00:17.693
We start with the neuromuscular blocking agents, comparing depolarizing and nondepolarizing drugs.

3
00:00:17.693 --> 00:00:22.778
Then the sedatives and the opioids, with their reversal agents.

4
00:00:22.778 --> 00:00:32.626
Then how drugs move through the body: half-life, routes of administration, bioavailability, and the organs of elimination.

5
00:00:32.626 --> 00:00:40.375
We finish with creatinine and BUN, the kidney tests that tell you whether a drug may accumulate.

6
00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:55.321
Neuromuscular blocking agents, or paralytics, cause skeletal muscle weakness or paralysis by interfering with acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction.

7
00:00:55.321 --> 00:01:00.062
Their site of action is the nicotinic receptors there.

8
00:01:00.062 --> 00:01:04.364
They do not sedate, and they do not relieve pain.

9
00:01:04.364 --> 00:01:14.900
So the order is analgesia, then sedation, then the paralytic, and a paralyzed patient must be on mechanical ventilation.

10
00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:18.038
There are two kinds.

11
00:01:18.038 --> 00:01:22.121
Succinylcholine is the only depolarizing agent.

12
00:01:22.121 --> 00:01:34.459
It is an acetylcholine receptor agonist that resists acetylcholinesterase, so it causes sustained depolarization, with visible fasciculations.

13
00:01:34.459 --> 00:01:43.755
Onset is sixty to ninety seconds, and it lasts ten to fifteen minutes, which makes it ideal for intubation.

14
00:01:43.755 --> 00:01:51.054
Hess gives thirty to sixty seconds and four to five minutes; use the deck's numbers.

15
00:01:51.054 --> 00:01:59.481
The nondepolarizing agents are vecuronium, rocuronium, pancuronium, cisatracurium and mivacurium.

16
00:01:59.481 --> 00:02:09.126
They are competitive antagonists that block the receptor without activating it, so there are no fasciculations.

17
00:02:09.126 --> 00:02:14.599
They take longer; Chang gives an onset of three to ten minutes.

18
00:02:14.599 --> 00:02:21.550
Pancuronium lasts longest and mivacurium is shortest, and all are given IV only.

19
00:02:22.967 --> 00:02:33.187
Succinylcholine is broken down by plasma cholinesterase, so pseudocholinesterase deficiency causes prolonged paralysis.

20
00:02:33.187 --> 00:02:43.235
Its hazards are hyperkalemia; raised intracranial, intraocular and intragastric pressure; and malignant hyperthermia.

21
00:02:43.235 --> 00:02:46.498
There is no reversal; you ride it out.

22
00:02:46.498 --> 00:03:02.558
Among the nondepolarizers, pancuronium is cleared by the kidneys, vecuronium by the liver, and cisatracurium by Hofmann degradation, which makes it the choice in hepatic or renal failure.

23
00:03:02.558 --> 00:03:06.938
Pancuronium can cause tachycardia and hypertension.

24
00:03:06.938 --> 00:03:16.042
The nondepolarizers are reversed with neostigmine, and sugammadex reverses only rocuronium and vecuronium.

25
00:03:17.467 --> 00:03:18.958
Pause and choose.

26
00:03:18.958 --> 00:03:26.501
A patient in both renal and hepatic failure needs ongoing paralysis on the ventilator.

27
00:03:26.501 --> 00:03:33.342
Which neuromuscular blocker do you recommend, and which ones should you avoid?

28
00:03:34.267 --> 00:03:40.267
[6-second practice pause]

29
00:03:40.767 --> 00:03:41.944
Cisatracurium.

30
00:03:41.944 --> 00:03:49.261
It is eliminated by Hofmann degradation, so it does not depend on the liver or kidneys.

31
00:03:49.261 --> 00:03:57.502
Avoid pancuronium, which is cleared by the kidneys, and vecuronium, which is cleared by the liver.

32
00:03:57.502 --> 00:04:04.062
The deck also says to avoid succinylcholine when the liver or kidneys are bad.

33
00:04:04.062 --> 00:04:10.117
Hess says no succinylcholine dose reduction is needed, but use the deck.

34
00:04:11.533 --> 00:04:20.051
Sedation is diminished awareness; analgesia is pain relief; and a sedative is not an analgesic.

35
00:04:20.051 --> 00:04:25.520
Benzodiazepines, barbiturates and propofol work through GABA.

36
00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:31.706
The benzodiazepines are midazolam, or Versed, lorazepam and diazepam.

37
00:04:31.706 --> 00:04:37.265
They are anxiolytic and amnestic, and they terminate seizures.

38
00:04:37.265 --> 00:04:44.886
Alone they do not change respiration, but they augment opioid respiratory depression.

39
00:04:44.886 --> 00:04:50.176
Midazolam is the most important for respiratory therapists.

40
00:04:50.176 --> 00:04:55.286
They give no analgesia, and their reversal is flumazenil.

41
00:04:55.286 --> 00:05:02.548
Propofol is IV only, with rapid onset and short action, so patients wake quickly.

42
00:05:02.548 --> 00:05:16.535
Its hazards are apnea, hypotension, raised triglycerides from its lipid vehicle, and propofol infusion syndrome above five milligrams per kilogram per hour.

43
00:05:16.535 --> 00:05:22.183
Hess says it gives no analgesia; Chang says only at high doses.

44
00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:24.590
Three more.

45
00:05:24.590 --> 00:05:30.441
Dexmedetomidine, or Precedex, is an alpha two adrenergic agonist.

46
00:05:30.441 --> 00:05:42.143
It gives sedation, anxiolysis and analgesia without respiratory depression, so it can be used before, during and after extubation.

47
00:05:42.143 --> 00:05:52.045
Etomidate is used for rapid sequence intubation: onset thirty to sixty seconds, lasting three to five minutes.

48
00:05:52.045 --> 00:05:58.076
It is the hemodynamically stable choice, but it gives no analgesia.

49
00:05:58.076 --> 00:06:10.949
Ketamine gives analgesia, amnesia and sedation while preserving respiratory drive, which makes it a good choice in hypotension or bronchospasm.

50
00:06:10.949 --> 00:06:18.150
No reversal agent is named for propofol, dexmedetomidine, etomidate or ketamine.

51
00:06:19.567 --> 00:06:29.049
Opioids are narcotic analgesics for moderate to severe pain, and they bind receptors in the brain and spinal cord.

52
00:06:29.049 --> 00:06:44.602
Morphine is the standard reference; fentanyl is very potent and fast acting; hydromorphone, or Dilaudid, is five to ten times stronger than morphine; and codeine and oxycodone are others.

53
00:06:44.602 --> 00:06:54.251
The deck marks respiratory depression as the most important side effect, and benzodiazepines plus opioids deepen it.

54
00:06:54.251 --> 00:07:00.239
Others are constipation, nausea and vomiting, and an antitussive effect.

55
00:07:00.239 --> 00:07:05.979
Doses that depress breathing have minimal effect on cardiac function.

56
00:07:05.979 --> 00:07:14.047
The Chang deck lists mu, kappa and sigma receptors; Hess lists mu, delta and kappa; use the deck.

57
00:07:14.047 --> 00:07:19.952
The reversal is naloxone, or Narcan, which also comes as a nasal spray.

58
00:07:19.952 --> 00:07:29.767
Opioids are given as needed by the nurse or by patient-controlled analgesia, with a bag-mask and naloxone in the room.

59
00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:32.605
Pause and think.

60
00:07:32.605 --> 00:07:40.244
A post-op patient received midazolam and morphine, and now breathes six times a minute.

61
00:07:40.244 --> 00:07:45.775
What explains this, and which reversal agent matches each drug?

62
00:07:46.700 --> 00:07:52.700
[6-second practice pause]

63
00:07:53.200 --> 00:08:01.940
Opioids cause respiratory depression, and benzodiazepines augment it, so the combination deepens it.

64
00:08:01.940 --> 00:08:08.058
Naloxone reverses the morphine, and flumazenil reverses the midazolam.

65
00:08:08.058 --> 00:08:15.575
This is why a bag-mask and naloxone stay in the room for patient-controlled analgesia.

66
00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:28.727
Plasma half-life is the time required for the plasma concentration of a drug to decrease by one half, a measure of how quickly a drug is eliminated.

67
00:08:28.727 --> 00:08:32.610
The longer the half-life, the longer the effects.

68
00:08:32.610 --> 00:08:43.386
Older adults and patients with impaired liver or kidney function may be at greater risk for toxicity, because their half-life is longer.

69
00:08:43.386 --> 00:08:53.132
Half-life also sets dosing frequency: a short half-life needs several doses a day, and a long one allows once-daily dosing.

70
00:08:53.132 --> 00:09:14.606
Some printed numbers: adenosine, about twelve seconds; omalizumab, seventeen to twenty two days; benzodiazepine sleep aids, over twelve to twenty four hours, the hangover; and diazepam, about twenty four hours in a younger adult, approaching ninety six in an older adult.

71
00:09:14.606 --> 00:09:21.975
Lorazepam lasts much longer than midazolam, and propofol's half-life is under thirty minutes.

72
00:09:23.400 --> 00:09:39.458
Carboxyhemoglobin has a half-life of about four to five hours on room air, about sixty to ninety minutes on one hundred percent oxygen, and often about twenty to thirty minutes with hyperbaric oxygen.

73
00:09:39.458 --> 00:09:43.633
Hess's Table 57-7 covers the neuromuscular blockers.

74
00:09:43.633 --> 00:10:01.217
Succinylcholine has the shortest onset and duration, one to one and a half minutes and five to ten minutes, and its half-life is listed as unknown; for the duration, the deck's ten to fifteen minutes is the exam answer.

75
00:10:01.217 --> 00:10:04.509
Rocuronium's onset is one to two minutes.

76
00:10:04.509 --> 00:10:24.100
Mivacurium's half-life is just two minutes, cisatracurium's twenty to thirty, vecuronium's sixty to one hundred thirty, and pancuronium's one hundred ten to one hundred forty, with a duration of one hundred twenty to one hundred eighty minutes.

77
00:10:25.500 --> 00:10:27.826
The deck lists five routes.

78
00:10:27.826 --> 00:10:38.250
Enteral, literally small intestine, is most commonly by mouth: tablets, capsules, suppositories, elixirs and suspensions.

79
00:10:38.250 --> 00:10:47.727
Parenteral, meaning beside the intestine, is by injection: IV, IM, subcutaneous, intraosseous and intrathecal.

80
00:10:47.727 --> 00:10:50.484
Transdermal is a patch or paste.

81
00:10:50.484 --> 00:10:55.911
Inhalation is a gas or aerosol, for a local or systemic effect.

82
00:10:55.911 --> 00:10:59.357
Topical is a powder, lotion or ointment.

83
00:10:59.357 --> 00:11:11.160
The inhaled route has advantages: a smaller dose, fewer side effects, rapid onset, it targets the respiratory system, and it is painless.

84
00:11:11.160 --> 00:11:23.652
Hess adds that inhalation bypasses the GI system and eliminates first-pass metabolism, and that lung deposition varies from one to fifty percent.

85
00:11:23.652 --> 00:11:31.750
Hess classes intrathecal as percutaneous or mucosal, and the deck as parenteral; use the deck.

86
00:11:33.167 --> 00:11:40.405
Bioavailability indicates the proportion of a drug that reaches the systemic circulation.

87
00:11:40.405 --> 00:11:53.743
It is lowered by inactivation from stomach acids and by metabolic degradation, and Hess measures it against the IV route, which is one hundred percent bioavailable.

88
00:11:53.743 --> 00:12:04.560
The first-pass effect: an oral drug that the liver metabolizes heavily has much of its activity ended as it passes through the liver.

89
00:12:04.560 --> 00:12:16.028
High first-pass metabolism lowers bioavailability, so the drug may need higher doses or another route, such as sublingual, transdermal or IV.

90
00:12:16.028 --> 00:12:25.381
Hess adds that absorption depends on drug pH, food, which slows it, fat solubility, contact time and concentration.

91
00:12:25.381 --> 00:12:42.867
For inhaled drugs, the L to T ratio is lung dose over lung plus GI dose; a higher ratio means more efficient lung delivery, and it rises with mouth rinsing, a reservoir device, patient instruction and a breath hold.

92
00:12:44.300 --> 00:12:52.794
The liver is a primary site of drug metabolism, and the kidneys are the primary site of drug excretion.

93
00:12:52.794 --> 00:13:05.577
Hess puts it this way: the kidney and, to a lesser extent, the liver are the major organs of elimination, and the lungs, saliva and sweat are minor routes.

94
00:13:05.577 --> 00:13:14.072
Elimination depends primarily on renal function, measured by creatinine clearance or the estimated GFR.

95
00:13:14.072 --> 00:13:24.298
Metabolism converts a drug to a water-soluble, inactive form; a drug that is not metabolized can accumulate to toxic levels.

96
00:13:24.298 --> 00:13:30.978
Renal failure reduces drug clearance and causes toxicity, the retention of drugs.

97
00:13:30.978 --> 00:13:46.400
That is why the paralytic choice depends on the organs: in renal failure avoid pancuronium, with hepatic problems avoid vecuronium, and if the liver or kidneys are bad, use cisatracurium.

98
00:13:47.800 --> 00:13:54.512
Both creatinine and BUN rise in kidney failure, and a rise in both is azotemia.

99
00:13:54.512 --> 00:14:01.393
Creatinine is a waste product of muscle metabolism, so it relates to muscle mass.

100
00:14:01.393 --> 00:14:14.647
It is filtered with minimal reabsorption, which makes it reliable for GFR and more specific for kidney function, and it runs lower in women and the elderly.

101
00:14:14.647 --> 00:14:22.633
BUN, or blood urea nitrogen, comes from urea, formed in the liver when protein is broken down.

102
00:14:22.633 --> 00:14:27.986
It is readily filtered, and Hess says about half is reabsorbed.

103
00:14:27.986 --> 00:14:39.625
It reflects protein metabolism and hydration, so it is less specific, and it rises with a high-protein diet, dehydration and GI bleeding.

104
00:14:41.033 --> 00:14:43.451
Use the renal deck's normals.

105
00:14:43.451 --> 00:14:56.374
Creatinine is zero point seven to one point three milligrams per deciliter in men and zero point six to one point one in women, and BUN is seven to twenty.

106
00:14:56.374 --> 00:15:02.544
Other decks and Hess print slightly different ranges, so stick with these.

107
00:15:02.544 --> 00:15:08.714
The BUN to creatinine ratio is normally about ten to one to twenty to one.

108
00:15:08.714 --> 00:15:20.553
A high BUN with a normal creatinine points to dehydration or a high protein intake; both high points to kidney disease or acute kidney injury.

109
00:15:20.553 --> 00:15:30.808
Hess adds a caution: creatinine is relatively insensitive, and may not rise until more than half of renal function is lost.

110
00:15:32.233 --> 00:15:33.079
Last one.

111
00:15:33.079 --> 00:15:41.531
A patient's BUN is forty two milligrams per deciliter, and the creatinine is one point oh.

112
00:15:41.531 --> 00:15:46.508
What is the ratio, and what does the pattern suggest?

113
00:15:47.433 --> 00:15:53.433
[6-second practice pause]

114
00:15:53.933 --> 00:16:00.344
The ratio is forty two to one, well above the normal ten to twenty to one.

115
00:16:00.344 --> 00:16:12.384
The BUN is high and the creatinine is normal, which points to dehydration or a high protein intake, and GI bleeding can also raise the BUN.

116
00:16:12.384 --> 00:16:21.133
If both were high, you would think kidney disease, and drugs cleared by the kidneys could accumulate.

117
00:16:22.533 --> 00:16:24.870
Here is part two in brief.

118
00:16:24.870 --> 00:16:38.168
Paralytics block acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction but do not sedate or relieve pain, so give analgesia and sedation first, and ventilate.

119
00:16:38.168 --> 00:16:46.974
Succinylcholine is the only depolarizer: fast, short, with no reversal, and it risks hyperkalemia.

120
00:16:46.974 --> 00:16:51.916
Cisatracurium is the choice in liver or kidney failure.

121
00:16:51.916 --> 00:17:01.710
Midazolam is reversed by flumazenil and opioids by naloxone, and together they deepen respiratory depression.

122
00:17:01.710 --> 00:17:07.460
A long half-life means longer effects and more risk of toxicity.

123
00:17:07.460 --> 00:17:11.594
The liver metabolizes and the kidneys excrete.

124
00:17:11.594 --> 00:17:20.669
And a high BUN with a normal creatinine points to dehydration, while both high points to the kidneys.

125
00:17:20.669 --> 00:17:24.083
Part three covers dosing and delivery.
